'' =================================================================================================
''
''   File....... SD_RT_error_injection_tests.spin2
''   Purpose.... This object tests the targeted fault-injection facility itself
''   Author..... Stephen M Moraco
''               -- see below for terms of use
''   E-mail..... stephen@ironsheep.biz
''   Started.... JUL 2026
''   Updated.... 12 AUG 2026
''
'' =================================================================================================
''
'' Every failure-path fix in this driver is proven by making a specific sector I/O
'' operation fail on demand. That makes the injection facility an instrument, and an
'' instrument has to be calibrated before anything measured with it can be believed.
'' This suite is that calibration:
''
''   - setTestFailSector(sector, mode) - fail one named LBA, read and/or write,
''     one-shot or sticky
''   - setTestFailWriteAfter(n) - fail the nth subsequent writeSector() call
''   - setTestFailReadAfter(n) - fail the nth subsequent card read (cache misses)
''   - getTestReadCallCount() - card reads counted since the read hook last reset
''   - getTestWriteCallCount() - how many writes an operation actually made
''   - clearTestErrors() - disarm everything, leaving no residue
''
'' Test groups:
''   - Disarmed Behavior (the facility is invisible until armed)
''   - Targeted Read Failure (sticky fails, one-shot recovers via retry)
''   - Targeted Write Failure (named LBA, and nth-write-in-a-sequence)
''   - Arming Precision (direction discrimination, untouched LBAs, clean disarm)
''   - Primitive Failure Reporting (the instrument turned on the driver: a failed
''     FAT read must reach the caller as an error instead of a stale chain walk)
''   - Tier-1 Silent Corruption Paths (one failed metadata write per test; the
''     assertion is that the on-disk state afterward is the recoverable one)
''   - Search Failure Is Not Not-Found (an unreadable directory must not read as
''     "the name is available", which would create a duplicate entry)
''   - Short Count Is Not End Of File (a read or write that stops part-way returns a
''     POSITIVE partial count; handleError() is what tells it from a clean stop)
''   - A Background Flush Failure Reaches Somebody (the idle auto-flush has no caller
''     to fail on, so lastFlushError() is the only way its failure is ever seen)
''   - Operations That Could Not Fail, Now Can (sync(), freeSpace() and stop() each
''     computed a status and then discarded it; all three now report)
''
'' SLOW-RUNNING: the last group waits out the driver's 200ms idle window several times.
'' Those waits are the condition under test and cannot be shortened.
''
'' SAFETY: every raw write in this suite writes a sector's own contents back to it,
'' read moments earlier. If an injection fails to fire - the very defect these tests
'' would be catching - the card is rewritten with the bytes it already held.
''
'' KNOWN RESIDUE (by design): Test 13 fails a FAT read mid-delete, and the correct
'' on-disk outcome it asserts is the recoverable one - directory entry already marked
'' deleted, cluster chain still allocated. The recovery pass cannot reattach that
'' chain (the entry is gone), so each run strands the test file's ~2 clusters as a
'' lost chain. This is intentional: repairing it here would mean a FAT edit under the
'' very cache the injection just poisoned. SEQUENCING: the regression runner's
'' sweep-start fsck reclaims lost chains, so the leak never accumulates across sweeps.
'' A standalone run of this suite leaves the ~2 clusters stranded until the next
'' fsck or reformat - harmless residue, invisible to every other suite.
''
'' =================================================================================================

CON

     _CLKFREQ        = 350_000_000

     ' SD card pin assignments
     ' Default: P2 Edge module SD slot. Build with -D SD_PINS_EXTERNAL
     ' to use the external SD header (base pin 16).
#ifdef SD_PINS_EXTERNAL
     SD_CS   = 20
     SD_MOSI = 19
     SD_MISO = 18
     SD_SCK  = 21
#else
     SD_CS   = 60
     SD_MOSI = 59
     SD_MISO = 58
     SD_SCK  = 61
#endif
     BUFFER_SIZE = 512

     MAX_READ_CRC_RETRIES = 3            ' Must match driver constant

     ' An LBA the driver can never reach on any card we test with. Arming here
     ' proves an armed-but-untouched injection stays out of the way.
     UNREACHABLE_LBA = $7FFF_FFFF

     WRITE_DIAG_CRC_REJECT = 3           ' getWriteDiag() result code for a rejected data block

     ' Comfortably past the driver's IDLE_FLUSH_MS (200), so the worker's idle window
     ' opens and the automatic flush actually runs. No command may be issued during
     ' this wait -- any command resets the idle timer and the flush never fires.
     IDLE_FLUSH_WAIT_MS = 400

' Enable all features (fault injection lives behind SD_INCLUDE_TEST_HOOKS)
#ifdef __SPINTOOLS__
#define SD_INCLUDE_ALL
#elseifdef __FLEXSPIN__
#define SD_INCLUDE_ALL
#pragma exportdef SD_INCLUDE_ALL
#else
#pragma exportdef SD_INCLUDE_ALL
#endif

OBJ
    sd    : "micro_sd_fat32_fs"
    utils : "isp_rt_utilities"

DAT

' Test filenames
injFile         BYTE    "INJECT1.BIN", 0
injMvSrc        BYTE    "INJMVSRC.TXT", 0        ' Test 34: source file for the aborted-move ghost test
injMvDir        BYTE    "INJMVD", 0              ' Test 34: destination directory
injMvChild      BYTE    "INJMVD/INJMVSRC.TXT", 0 ' Test 34: where the ghost entry would appear
mvDestChild     BYTE    "TIER1MV/MOVESRC.BIN", 0 ' Test 20: where a regressed moveFile leaves the copy
crossA          BYTE    "CROSSA.BIN", 0
crossB          BYTE    "CROSSB.BIN", 0
renFile         BYTE    "RENSRC.BIN", 0
renNewName      BYTE    "RENDST.BIN", 0
mvSrcFile       BYTE    "MOVESRC.BIN", 0
dirGood         BYTE    "TIER1GD", 0
dirBad          BYTE    "TIER1BD", 0
dirFull         BYTE    "TIER1FL", 0
dirMoveDest     BYTE    "TIER1MV", 0
rootPath        BYTE    "/", 0
extDirName      BYTE    "EXTDIR", 0              ' Test 36: subdirectory driven to a cluster boundary
extWitName      BYTE    "EXTWIT.TMP", 0          ' Test 36: boundary-witness create (all three attempts)
fillEntryName   BYTE    "ZZFILLER   ", 0         ' Test 36: 11-byte 8.3 name field for synthetic fill entries

' Buffers
writeBuffer     BYTE    0[BUFFER_SIZE]
readBuffer      BYTE    0[BUFFER_SIZE]
readBuffer_guard BYTE   0[16]
sectorBuffer    BYTE    0[BUFFER_SIZE]
sectorBuffer_guard BYTE 0[16]
compareBuffer   BYTE    0[BUFFER_SIZE]

' A request spanning two sectors, so one sector can succeed and the next fail
' inside a SINGLE readHandle()/writeHandle() call - which is what produces a
' partial count rather than a clean zero.
spanBuffer      BYTE    0[BUFFER_SIZE * 2]
spanBuffer_guard BYTE   0[16]

' Throwaway receivers for debugGetReadSectorDiag(); only the sector is wanted.
diagLastSector  LONG    0
diagScratch     LONG    0[7]

PUB go() | result, handle, bytesRead, bytesWritten, errCount, testSector, mismatchBefore, mismatchAfter, retryBefore, retryAfter, diagResult, diagR1, diagResp, diagSector, writeCalls, freeBefore, freeAfter, nameMatches, handleB, dataSector, secPerClus, entriesN
'' Main entry point for fault-injection facility regression tests

    debug(" ")
    debug("==============================================")
    debug("  SD Card Driver - Error Injection Tests")
    debug("==============================================")

    ' Mount the card first
    result := sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    ifnot utils.requireSetup(result == 0, @"SETUP: mount SD card")
        debug("ERROR: Could not mount SD card!")
        utils.ShowTestEndCounts()
        debug("END_SESSION")
        return

    ' A sector at mid-card: well past FAT32 metadata, and every write below
    ' writes back the content just read from it.
    testSector := sd.cardSizeSectors() / 2
    debug("   Test sector (mid-card): ", udec(testSector))
    debug("   Dummy-CRC card: ", udec((sd.cardWarnings() & sd.CW_NO_DATA_CRC) <> 0))

    ' Start from a known-disarmed state, with no fixture residue from an
    ' aborted prior run (an interrupted suite never reaches its teardown).
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    cleanupTestItems()

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Disarmed Behavior
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Disarmed Behavior")

    ' Test 1: With nothing armed, a full file cycle behaves exactly as it does
    ' in a build that has no injection code at all.
    utils.startTest(@"Disarmed facility leaves a full file cycle untouched")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $10)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    bytesWritten := 0
    bytesRead := 0
    if handle >= 0
        bytesWritten := sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@injFile)
    if handle >= 0
        bytefill(@readBuffer, 0, BUFFER_SIZE)
        utils.initGuard(@readBuffer_guard)
        bytesRead := sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        utils.checkGuard(@readBuffer_guard, @"readBuffer guard")
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesWritten, @"write returns 512 bytes", BUFFER_SIZE)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesRead, @"read returns 512 bytes", BUFFER_SIZE)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(utils.verifyBufferPattern(@readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $10), @"data reads back intact", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(errCount, @"no errors injected", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 2: A disarmed raw read of the test sector succeeds. This also captures
    ' the sector's true contents, which every later write in this suite writes back.
    utils.startTest(@"Disarmed raw read succeeds and captures baseline content")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(2)
    utils.initGuard(@sectorBuffer_guard)
    result := sd.readSectorRaw(testSector, @sectorBuffer)
    utils.checkGuard(@sectorBuffer_guard, @"sectorBuffer guard")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"raw read returns SUCCESS", 0)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestErrorCount(), @"no errors injected", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Targeted Read Failure
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Targeted Read Failure")

    ' Test 3: A sticky read failure at a named LBA fails every retry and surfaces
    ' as E_CRC_ERROR - the identical outcome of a genuine CRC failure, reached by
    ' the identical code path (the driver's own retry loop and mismatch counters).
    utils.startTest(@"Sticky read injection returns E_CRC_ERROR via the real retry path")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    mismatchBefore := sd.getCRCMismatchCount()
    retryBefore := sd.getCRCRetryCount()
    sd.setTestFailSector(testSector, sd.TF_READ | sd.TF_STICKY)
    result := sd.readSectorRaw(testSector, @readBuffer)
    mismatchAfter := sd.getCRCMismatchCount()
    retryAfter := sd.getCRCRetryCount()
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    debug("  result=", sdec_(result), " mismatches ", udec_(mismatchBefore), "->", udec_(mismatchAfter), " retries ", udec_(retryBefore), "->", udec_(retryAfter))
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"raw read returns E_CRC_ERROR", sd.E_CRC_ERROR)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(mismatchAfter > mismatchBefore, @"CRC mismatch counter increased", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(retryAfter > retryBefore, @"CRC retry counter increased", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(errCount, @"one injection per attempt", MAX_READ_CRC_RETRIES)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 4: Clearing the injection restores the sector. The data must match the
    ' baseline captured in test 2 - a failed read must not have disturbed content.
    utils.startTest(@"Clearing a sticky read injection restores the sector")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(2)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    bytefill(@compareBuffer, 0, BUFFER_SIZE)
    result := sd.readSectorRaw(testSector, @compareBuffer)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"raw read returns SUCCESS", 0)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestErrorCount(), @"no errors injected after clear", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 5: A failed read must not have disturbed what is on the card. Compare
    ' against the baseline captured in test 2.
    utils.startTest(@"Injected read failure left the sector content untouched")
    utils.evaluateBufferMatch(@compareBuffer, @sectorBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, @"content matches pre-injection baseline")

    ' Test 6: A one-shot read failure is consumed by the first attempt, so the
    ' driver's retry recovers and the caller sees a successful read. This is the
    ' transient-error case, and it is what distinguishes one-shot from sticky.
    utils.startTest(@"One-shot read injection is recovered by the driver retry")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    retryBefore := sd.getCRCRetryCount()
    sd.setTestFailSector(testSector, sd.TF_READ)
    result := sd.readSectorRaw(testSector, @readBuffer)
    retryAfter := sd.getCRCRetryCount()
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    debug("  result=", sdec_(result), " retries ", udec_(retryBefore), "->", udec_(retryAfter), " errCount=", udec_(errCount))
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"raw read returns SUCCESS after retry", 0)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(retryAfter > retryBefore, @"a retry was actually taken", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(errCount, @"injection consumed exactly once", 1)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Targeted Write Failure
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Targeted Write Failure")

    ' Test 7: A write failure at a named LBA reports E_WRITE_REJECTED and records
    ' the same write-diagnostic code (3) a card sets when it rejects a data block.
    ' The buffer written is the sector's own content, so nothing is at risk.
    utils.startTest(@"Write injection returns E_WRITE_REJECTED with a reject diagnostic")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.setTestFailSector(testSector, sd.TF_WRITE)
    result := sd.writeSectorRaw(testSector, @sectorBuffer)
    diagResult, diagR1, diagResp, diagSector := sd.getWriteDiag()
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    debug("  result=", sdec_(result), " diagResult=", udec_(diagResult), " diagSector=", udec_(diagSector))
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"raw write returns E_WRITE_REJECTED", sd.E_WRITE_REJECTED)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(diagResult, @"write diagnostic reports a reject", WRITE_DIAG_CRC_REJECT)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(diagSector, @"diagnostic names the injected sector", testSector)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(errCount, @"injection consumed exactly once", 1)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 8: The one-shot write injection is spent, so the same write now lands.
    utils.startTest(@"Write succeeds once the one-shot injection is spent")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(2)
    result := sd.writeSectorRaw(testSector, @sectorBuffer)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"raw write returns SUCCESS", 0)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestErrorCount(), @"no further injection", 1)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 9: setTestFailWriteAfter names a write by its position in a sequence
    ' rather than by LBA - how a specific write inside a multi-write operation
    ' (a FAT2 mirror update, a directory rewrite) is made to fail on its own.
    utils.startTest(@"setTestFailWriteAfter fails the nth write, not the first")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    ' Flush first: sync() synchronously writes out every dirty handle and FSInfo
    ' (the same pass unmount runs), so nothing is left for the idle auto-flush to
    ' write mid-test and be counted against the arming below. Every bare sd.sync()
    ' before an arming in this suite is this same barrier.
    sd.sync()
    sd.setTestFailWriteAfter(2)
    result := sd.writeSectorRaw(testSector, @sectorBuffer)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"first write succeeds", 0)
    result := sd.writeSectorRaw(testSector, @sectorBuffer)
    writeCalls := sd.getTestWriteCallCount()
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"second write returns E_WRITE_REJECTED", sd.E_WRITE_REJECTED)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(writeCalls, @"two write calls counted", 2)
    result := sd.writeSectorRaw(testSector, @sectorBuffer)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"third write succeeds (one-shot spent)", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Arming Precision
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Arming Precision")

    ' Test 10: An injection armed on a sector nothing touches has no effect on
    ' anything else. Armed for both directions and sticky - the loudest setting.
    utils.startTest(@"Injection armed on an untouched LBA affects nothing")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.setTestFailSector(UNREACHABLE_LBA, sd.TF_BOTH | sd.TF_STICKY)
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $20)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    bytesWritten := 0
    bytesRead := 0
    if handle >= 0
        bytesWritten := sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@injFile)
    if handle >= 0
        bytefill(@readBuffer, 0, BUFFER_SIZE)
        bytesRead := sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesWritten, @"write returns 512 bytes", BUFFER_SIZE)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesRead, @"read returns 512 bytes", BUFFER_SIZE)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(utils.verifyBufferPattern(@readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $20), @"data reads back intact", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(errCount, @"nothing injected", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 11: Direction is honored - a read-armed LBA does not fail writes to it.
    utils.startTest(@"TF_READ arming does not fail a write to the same LBA")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(2)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.setTestFailSector(testSector, sd.TF_READ | sd.TF_STICKY)
    result := sd.writeSectorRaw(testSector, @sectorBuffer)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"write to a read-armed LBA succeeds", 0)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestErrorCount(), @"write consumed no injection", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 12: clearTestErrors leaves no residue at all - no armed sector, no
    ' pending write countdown, no stale counters for the next suite to trip over.
    utils.startTest(@"clearTestErrors leaves no residue")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(5)
    sd.setTestFailSector(testSector, sd.TF_BOTH | sd.TF_STICKY)
    sd.setTestFailWriteAfter(1)
    sd.setTestForceReadError(5)
    sd.setTestForceWriteError(1)
    sd.setTestMaxClusters(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestErrorCount(), @"error count zeroed", 0)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestWriteCallCount(), @"write call count zeroed", 0)
    result := sd.readSectorRaw(testSector, @compareBuffer)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"read succeeds after clear", 0)
    result := sd.writeSectorRaw(testSector, @sectorBuffer)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"write succeeds after clear", 0)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestErrorCount(), @"still nothing injected", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Primitive Failure Reporting
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Primitive Failure Reporting")

    ' The internal FAT-chain reader hands back a dereferenceable pointer even when
    ' the read failed, so the contents are whatever the previous read left in the
    ' buffer. Every caller now consults the companion status before trusting it.
    '
    ' Two arming details make these tests fire reliably:
    '   - Read injection fires only on a cache MISS, so each test unmounts and
    '     remounts first; mount invalidates the FAT buffer.
    '   - A one-shot read injection is recovered by the driver's own retry, so
    '     these must be TF_STICKY to reach the caller as a failure.
    '
    ' The armed LBA is the first FAT sector, which holds the entries for the low
    ' cluster numbers a freshly written test file receives. The "injection fired"
    ' sub-check is what keeps a miss from reading as a pass.

    ' Test 13: A failed FAT read during delete must abort the chain walk. Walking a
    ' stale link frees clusters belonging to OTHER files while reporting success --
    ' the single most destructive outcome the audit found.
    utils.startTest(@"Failed FAT read aborts delete instead of freeing a stale chain")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $30)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    bytesWritten := 0
    if handle >= 0
        ' Write past one cluster so the file owns a chain, not a single link
        repeat ((sd.clusterBytes() * 2) / BUFFER_SIZE)
            bytesWritten += sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(bytesWritten > sd.clusterBytes(), @"test file spans more than one cluster", true)

    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    freeBefore := sd.freeSpace()
    sd.setTestFailSector(sd.debugGetFatSec(), sd.TF_READ | sd.TF_STICKY)
    result := sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    freeAfter := sd.freeSpace()

    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"FAT read injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"deleteFile reports the I/O error", true)
    ' The walk aborts before zeroing anything, so not one cluster is handed back.
    utils.evaluateSubValue(freeAfter, @"no clusters freed from a stale chain", freeBefore)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Recover the card: the entry may already be marked deleted with its clusters
    ' still allocated, which is the correct on-disk outcome above (an undeleted
    ' file is recoverable; a chain freed under a live entry is not).
    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)

    ' Test 14: the same failure reached through a different caller. A fragment count
    ' derived from a garbage chain is a number with no meaning, so it must not be
    ' returned as one.
    utils.startTest(@"Failed FAT read is reported by fileFragments, not counted")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $40)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    if handle >= 0
        repeat ((sd.clusterBytes() * 2) / BUFFER_SIZE)
            sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)

    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    result := sd.fileFragments(@injFile)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result > 0, @"fragment count is positive when healthy", true)

    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    sd.setTestFailSector(sd.debugGetFatSec(), sd.TF_READ | sd.TF_STICKY)
    result := sd.fileFragments(@injFile)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"FAT read injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"fileFragments reports the error", true)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Tier-1 Silent Corruption Paths
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Tier-1 Silent Corruption Paths")

    ' Each test below fails ONE write inside a metadata update that used to ignore
    ' its result. The assertion is never merely "an error was returned" -- it is
    ' that the on-disk state after the failure is the recoverable one. Reverting
    ' any one of the driver fixes turns its test here red.
    '
    ' These are WRITE injections, so unlike the read tests above they need no
    ' cache-miss dance: writeSector short-circuits before the card is touched.

    ' Test 15 (2.1): a failed FAT write must not report the cluster as allocated.
    ' If it does, the next allocation scan finds that cluster still free on the card
    ' and hands the SAME one to a second file -- two files over one chain.
    utils.startTest(@"Failed FAT write does not hand the same cluster to two files")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.deleteFile(@crossA)
    sd.deleteFile(@crossB)
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)                             ' Test 14 leaves INJECT1.BIN; without this the armed
                                                        ' createFileNew bails E_FILE_EXISTS BEFORE the FAT write
    sd.sync()
    sd.setTestFailSector(sd.debugGetFatSec(), sd.TF_WRITE)
    result := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"FAT write injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"createFileNew reports the I/O error", true)

    ' Now allocate twice for real. If the failed allocation had been reported as
    ' successful, one of these two files would be reading the other's data.
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $51)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@crossA)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $62)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@crossB)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    sd.sync()

    bytefill(@readBuffer, 0, BUFFER_SIZE)
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@crossA)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(utils.verifyBufferPattern(@readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $51), @"first file holds its own data", true)
    bytefill(@readBuffer, 0, BUFFER_SIZE)
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@crossB)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(utils.verifyBufferPattern(@readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $62), @"second file holds its own data", true)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.deleteFile(@crossA)
    sd.deleteFile(@crossB)

    ' Test 16 (2.2): a disk-full allocation returns E_DISK_FULL (-60). Unguarded,
    ' that negative reached clus2sec() and the driver issued sec_per_clus sector
    ' writes at a wild address derived from it.
    utils.startTest(@"Disk-full directory creation fails without a wild write")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.sync()
    sd.setTestMaxClusters(3)                            ' Far too few clusters to satisfy the request
    result := sd.newDirectory(@dirFull)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"newDirectory reports failure", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"reports E_DISK_FULL specifically", sd.E_DISK_FULL)
    ' The card must still be sound: a full create/read cycle works afterward.
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $73)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    bytesRead := 0
    if handle >= 0
        sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
        handle := sd.openFileRead(@injFile)
        if handle >= 0
            bytefill(@readBuffer, 0, BUFFER_SIZE)
            bytesRead := sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
            sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(bytesRead == BUFFER_SIZE AND utils.verifyBufferPattern(@readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $73), @"filesystem still sound afterward", true)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)

    ' Test 17 (2.3): if the entry-deleted write fails, the chain must NOT be freed.
    ' Entry live + clusters free is the unrecoverable combination: the file still
    ' appears in the directory while its clusters get handed to the next allocation.
    utils.startTest(@"Failed delete leaves the file intact, not half-deleted")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $84)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    sd.sync()
    sd.setTestFailSector(sd.debugGetRootSec(), sd.TF_WRITE)
    result := sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"entry-write injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"deleteFile reports the I/O error", true)
    bytefill(@readBuffer, 0, BUFFER_SIZE)
    bytesRead := 0
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@injFile)
    if handle >= 0
        bytesRead := sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(bytesRead == BUFFER_SIZE AND utils.verifyBufferPattern(@readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $84), @"file still opens and reads correctly", true)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)

    ' Test 18 (2.4): the ./.. write is what makes a new cluster a directory. If it
    ' fails, the parent must gain no entry pointing at that uninitialized cluster.
    ' The driver writes ./.. BEFORE the parent entry precisely so this holds.
    ' Self-calibrating: measure a healthy newDirectory, then fail its ./.. write,
    ' which is the second-to-last write of the operation.
    utils.startTest(@"Failed ./.. write leaves no parent entry for the directory")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    ' TIER1GD/TIER1BD are fresh names here: cleanupTestItems() at suite start
    ' removed any prior run's residue (a leftover TIER1GD would make this
    ' newDirectory return E_FILE_EXISTS with ZERO writes -- 3 sub-fails).
    sd.sync()                                           ' Flush first so no pending write is counted
    sd.clearTestErrors()                                ' Zeroes the write counter
    sd.newDirectory(@dirGood)
    writeCalls := sd.getTestWriteCallCount()            ' Sampled BEFORE any sync of our own
    utils.evaluateSubBool(writeCalls > 1, @"healthy newDirectory write count measured", true)

    sd.sync()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.setTestFailWriteAfter(writeCalls - 1)            ' The ./.. write, not the parent entry
    result := sd.newDirectory(@dirBad)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0 AND result < 0, @"injection fired and error reported", true)
    ' The decisive assertion: nothing in the parent refers to that cluster.
    utils.evaluateSubBool(sd.changeDirectory(@dirBad) < 0, @"parent has no entry for it", true)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)

    ' Test 19 (2.5): the rewritten 8.3 entry IS the whole product of a rename. If it
    ' does not land, the file keeps its old name -- so success must not be reported.
    utils.startTest(@"Failed rename reports the error and keeps the original name")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.deleteFile(@renFile)
    sd.deleteFile(@renNewName)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $95)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@renFile)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    sd.sync()
    sd.setTestFailSector(sd.debugGetRootSec(), sd.TF_WRITE)
    result := sd.rename(@renFile, @renNewName)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"rename-entry injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"rename reports the I/O error", true)
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@renFile)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(handle >= 0, @"file still findable under its ORIGINAL name", true)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.deleteFile(@renFile)
    sd.deleteFile(@renNewName)

    ' Test 20 (2.6): a move is a source delete plus a destination write. If the
    ' source delete fails the move must be abandoned -- one file live in two
    ' directories over one cluster chain means deleting either frees clusters the
    ' other still points at.
    utils.startTest(@"Failed move never leaves the file in two directories")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
    sd.deleteFile(@mvSrcFile)
    sd.newDirectory(@dirMoveDest)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $A6)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@mvSrcFile)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    sd.sync()
    sd.setTestFailSector(sd.debugGetRootSec(), sd.TF_WRITE)
    result := sd.moveFile(@mvSrcFile, @dirMoveDest)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"source-delete injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"moveFile reports the I/O error", true)
    ' Exactly one directory may hold it. The source is where it must still be.
    sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@mvSrcFile)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    bytesRead := handle
    sd.changeDirectory(@dirMoveDest)
    result := sd.openFileRead(@mvSrcFile)
    if result >= 0
        sd.closeFileHandle(result)
    sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(bytesRead >= 0 AND result < 0, @"present in source only, not both", true)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.deleteFile(@mvSrcFile)

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Search Failure Is Not "Not Found"
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Search Failure Is Not Not-Found")

    ' searchDirectory() returns FALSE both for "the name is not there" and for "the
    ' directory could not be read", and the companion field search_io_status is what
    ' separates them. The create paths are where conflating the two is destructive:
    ' FALSE read as "the name is available" makes the driver write a SECOND entry and
    ' allocate a SECOND chain for a file that already exists.

    ' Test 21: the decisive case. Ask to create a file that ALREADY EXISTS while the
    ' directory read is failing. Fail-open would create a duplicate; fail-closed
    ' reports the I/O error and leaves exactly one entry.
    utils.startTest(@"Failed existence check refuses to create a duplicate entry")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $B7)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    sd.sync()

    ' Remount, then DROP the directory cache so the armed read is a cache MISS.
    ' The remount alone is not enough: mount's volume-label walk primes dir_buf
    ' with root_sec, so without the invalidation the injection never fires.
    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    sd.syncDirCache()
    sd.setTestFailSector(sd.debugGetRootSec(), sd.TF_READ | sd.TF_STICKY)
    result := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"directory read injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"createFileNew reports an error", true)
    ' It must NOT be E_FILE_EXISTS either -- the check never completed, so the driver
    ' cannot claim to know the name was taken.
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result <> sd.E_FILE_EXISTS, @"error is the I/O failure, not FILE_EXISTS", true)

    ' The assertion the whole finding is about: one name, one entry.
    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    nameMatches := 0
    handle := sd.openDirectory(@rootPath)
    if handle >= 0
        repeat while sd.readDirectoryHandle(handle)
            if strcomp(sd.fileName(), @injFile)
                nameMatches++
        sd.closeDirectoryHandle(handle)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(nameMatches, @"exactly one directory entry for the name", 1)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 22: a genuinely absent file must still report E_FILE_NOT_FOUND, with the
    ' companion field clean. The fix must not turn every miss into an I/O error.
    utils.startTest(@"A genuinely absent file still reports FILE_NOT_FOUND")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(2)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    result := sd.openFileRead(@injFile)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"absent file reports E_FILE_NOT_FOUND", sd.E_FILE_NOT_FOUND)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestErrorCount(), @"nothing was injected", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Short Count Is Not End Of File
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Short Count Is Not End Of File")

    ' readHandle() and writeHandle() return a byte COUNT, and a failure part-way through
    ' a transfer returns the bytes moved so far. A partial count is POSITIVE, so the
    ' documented read loop
    '
    '     repeat while (n := sd.readHandle(h, @buf, 512)) > 0
    '
    ' ends identically on a card failure and on a clean end of file: a file truncated
    ' part-way through is processed as a complete one. handleError(handle) is what
    ' separates them, and these tests are the proof that it does.
    '
    ' A ZERO-BYTE FAILURE IS THE ONE CASE THAT DOES NOT WORK THIS WAY. When a transfer
    ' fails before moving a single byte there is no partial count to return, and a zero
    ' would BE the loop's stop condition -- so the driver returns the error code instead.
    ' Zero therefore means end of file and nothing else. Test 24 pins that down.
    '
    ' Arming a DATA sector needs its LBA, and no accessor reports one. Each read test
    ' below therefore DISCOVERS it: read the wanted offset once on a healthy card and
    ' ask debugGetReadSectorDiag() which sector that read touched. The remount that
    ' follows is required - a read injection fires only on a cache MISS - and the
    ' arming is TF_STICKY because the driver's own retry recovers a one-shot.
    '
    ' NOT COVERED HERE: the E_BAD_CHAIN metadata-region write guard. Reaching it means
    ' forging a FAT entry to point at cluster 0 or 1, which needs the file's cluster
    ' number, and the public API exposes no way to learn it. The guard is a
    ' correct-by-construction backstop that must never fire in a passing run; its
    ' reporting is proven by inspection, not by injection.

    ' Test 23: the control. Both clean stops - a full read and a genuine end of file -
    ' must report SUCCESS, or a test that looks for an error afterward proves nothing.
    utils.startTest(@"A complete read and a genuine EOF both report clean")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $5A)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    bytesWritten := 0
    if handle >= 0
        repeat 4                                        ' Four sectors: room to fail somewhere other than the first
            bytesWritten += sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    sd.sync()
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesWritten, @"test file is four sectors long", BUFFER_SIZE * 4)

    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@injFile)
    bytesRead := sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesRead, @"full sector read returns every byte", BUFFER_SIZE)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.handleError(handle), @"handleError clean after a good read", 0)

    ' Seek to the end, then ask for more. This is the stop the read loop is written
    ' around, and it must be indistinguishable from success.
    sd.seekHandle(handle, BUFFER_SIZE * 4)
    bytesRead := sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(bytesRead == 0 AND sd.handleError(handle) == 0, @"genuine EOF returns 0 and reports clean", true)
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 24: the finding itself, and the fix for it. A failure on the FIRST sector
    ' transfers nothing. Returning 0 there would hand back the exact value test 23 just
    ' established means "end of file", making a dying card and a finished file the same
    ' event to every caller. The driver returns the error code instead, so the count
    ' alone is now enough - and handleError() agrees with it rather than being the only
    ' way to find out.
    utils.startTest(@"A read failure at the first sector returns an error, not a zero that reads as EOF")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()

    dataSector := discoverDataSector(0)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(dataSector > sd.debugGetRootSec(), @"discovered a data-region LBA to arm", true)

    ' Remount so the read is a cache miss, then make that one sector fail for good.
    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    sd.setTestFailSector(dataSector, sd.TF_READ | sd.TF_STICKY)
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@injFile)
    bytesRead := sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
    result := sd.handleError(handle)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    sd.clearTestErrors()

    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"data sector injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesRead, @"nothing transferred, so the ERROR is returned, never a zero", sd.E_IO_ERROR)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"handleError agrees: the I/O failure, not EOF", sd.E_IO_ERROR)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 25: the same failure one sector later, inside a single two-sector request.
    ' Now the count is POSITIVE and short - a truncated file that a length check alone
    ' would accept as complete.
    utils.startTest(@"A read failure mid-request returns a partial count and says why")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()

    dataSector := discoverDataSector(BUFFER_SIZE)       ' The file's SECOND sector this time

    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    sd.setTestFailSector(dataSector, sd.TF_READ | sd.TF_STICKY)
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@injFile)
    bytesRead := sd.readHandle(handle, @spanBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE * 2)
    result := sd.handleError(handle)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    sd.clearTestErrors()

    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"second-sector injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesRead, @"first sector delivered, second refused", BUFFER_SIZE)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(bytesRead > 0, @"the short count is POSITIVE -- unreadable as an error", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"handleError reports the I/O failure", sd.E_IO_ERROR)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 26: the write path's existing contract must not change. When NOTHING has
    ' been accepted, writeHandle() still returns the negative code, and handleError()
    ' agrees with it rather than contradicting it.
    utils.startTest(@"A write that accepts nothing still returns the error code")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    ' Fill one sector exactly. Nothing reaches the card yet -- the handle buffer is
    ' dirty and the next call must flush it before it can accept anything.
    sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
    sd.setTestFailWriteAfter(1)                         ' The stale-buffer flush is the very next write
    bytesWritten := sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
    result := sd.handleError(handle)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)

    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"flush injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesWritten, @"zero accepted, so the return stays negative", sd.E_IO_ERROR)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"handleError agrees with the return value", sd.E_IO_ERROR)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 27: bytes accepted, then a failure. The count must be returned rather than
    ' an error -- the caller has to know how much of the file is valid -- which is
    ' exactly why the reason has to live somewhere else.
    utils.startTest(@"A write failure mid-request returns a partial count and says why")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@spanBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE * 2, $6C)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    sd.setTestFailWriteAfter(1)                         ' Fail the first sector's flush, after its bytes are counted
    bytesWritten := sd.writeHandle(handle, @spanBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE * 2)
    result := sd.handleError(handle)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)

    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"write injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesWritten, @"first sector counted, second never started", BUFFER_SIZE)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(bytesWritten > 0, @"the short count is POSITIVE -- unreadable as an error", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"handleError reports the I/O failure", sd.E_IO_ERROR)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 28: attribution. The whole reason this is per-handle rather than per-cog is
    ' that one cog can have several files open; a failure on one must not appear on
    ' another, and must not be erased by another handle's success.
    utils.startTest(@"handleError belongs to the handle, not the driver")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    sd.deleteFile(@crossA)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    handleB := sd.createFileNew(@crossA)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(handle >= 0 AND handleB >= 0 AND handle <> handleB, @"two distinct handles open", true)

    sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)   ' Dirty, nothing on the card yet
    sd.setTestFailWriteAfter(1)
    sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)   ' Its flush fails
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()

    ' A healthy write on the OTHER handle, AFTER the failure. If the slot were shared
    ' this would wipe the record of it.
    bytesWritten := sd.writeHandle(handleB, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)

    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"injection fired on the first handle", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.handleError(handle), @"failing handle still reports its error", sd.E_IO_ERROR)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.handleError(handleB), @"healthy handle reports clean", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    sd.closeFileHandle(handleB)

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: A Background Flush Failure Reaches Somebody
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"A Background Flush Failure Reaches Somebody")

    ' SLOW-RUNNING BY NECESSITY. Each test below has to let the driver sit idle past
    ' IDLE_FLUSH_MS with no command traffic, so it spends real time doing nothing. The
    ' waits cannot be shortened - they ARE the condition under test.
    '
    ' The idle flush is the path a caller's data takes when they write without ever
    ' calling sync. The worker starts it on its own, so there is no call of theirs for
    ' it to fail on: before lastFlushError() existed, a failure here meant the data was
    ' gone and every later API call still returned success.
    '
    ' Only pure accessors may be called during an idle window. Anything that issues a
    ' command resets the idle timer and the flush never fires.

    ' Test 29: the control. A healthy idle flush must leave the report clean, or an
    ' error seen in test 30 proves nothing.
    utils.startTest(@"A clean background flush reports no error")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.clearFlushError()
    sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    utils.fillBufferWithPattern(@writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE, $A3)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(handle >= 0, @"handle open for the flush to find", true)
    ' A partial sector leaves the handle dirty with nothing yet on the card.
    sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE / 2)
    waitms(IDLE_FLUSH_WAIT_MS)                          ' No commands -- let the worker flush on its own

    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.lastFlushError(), @"healthy flush leaves the report clean", 0)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestErrorCount(), @"nothing was injected", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)

    ' Test 30: the finding. Fail the flush's own write. Nobody is waiting on it, so
    ' without this field the loss is silent and permanent.
    utils.startTest(@"A failed background flush is reported, and the data is still retrievable")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(5)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.clearFlushError()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE / 2)
    sd.setTestFailWriteAfter(1)                         ' The flush's data write is the next one to happen
    waitms(IDLE_FLUSH_WAIT_MS)

    result := sd.lastFlushError()
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()                                ' Also zeroes the write counter

    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"flush injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"lastFlushError reports the I/O failure", sd.E_IO_ERROR)

    ' Reading must not clear it -- the whole point is that it survives until polled.
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.lastFlushError(), @"reading the report does not clear it", sd.E_IO_ERROR)

    ' The handle is still dirty, so the data is recoverable. A sync that actually
    ' writes proves it; a sync of a clean handle would make no card write at all.
    result := sd.syncHandle(handle)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result == 0 AND sd.getTestWriteCallCount() > 0, @"retry sync still had the data to write", true)

    sd.clearFlushError()
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.lastFlushError(), @"clearFlushError clears it", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)

    ' Test 31: ordinary command traffic must not manufacture a flush error. The report
    ' belongs to the automatic path alone -- a write that fails on a caller's own call
    ' is that call's business, and is reported to that caller.
    '
    ' NOTE: the abort branch INSIDE do_idle_flush(), where a command arrives between
    ' two handle syncs, is not directly reachable from a single test cog - it would
    ' have to issue a command while the worker is mid-flush. What is asserted here is
    ' the property that branch exists to protect: a flush that never gets to run
    ' because commands keep arriving leaves nothing behind.
    utils.startTest(@"Command traffic never manufactures a flush error")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.clearFlushError()
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    ' Keep the driver busy: every call resets the idle timer, so no flush window opens.
    repeat 8
        sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
    bytesWritten := sd.tellHandle(handle)

    utils.evaluateSubValue(bytesWritten, @"the writes went through normally", BUFFER_SIZE * 8)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.lastFlushError(), @"no flush ran, so nothing is reported", 0)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(sd.getTestErrorCount(), @"nothing was injected", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Operations That Could Not Fail, Now Can
    ' ======================================================================
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Operations That Could Not Fail, Now Can")

    ' Three methods used to report success no matter what happened underneath, because
    ' the status was computed and then thrown away. sync()'s dispatcher hardcoded
    ' SUCCESS while do_sync() swallowed both its read and its write; freeSpace()'s did
    ' the same while an aborted FAT scan returned a partial count as fact; stop()
    ' discarded the final unmount status and then halted the worker, making the failure
    ' permanently unobservable.

    ' Test 32: a failed handle sync must SAY so, and must keep the dirty data so a
    ' later sync can still land it. (RETARGETED 2026-08-09, v1.7.0 task #50: the old
    ' form armed the injection against sd.sync() after createFileNew, on the premise
    ' that the create leaves a directory entry pending for sync -- it does not:
    ' do_create writes the entry inline and never sets F_NEWDIR, so the armed write
    ' never fired, the "injection fired" check failed on every hardware run ever,
    ' and the retry check passed vacuously against a no-op sync. The reachable
    ' equivalent contract is syncHandle over a dirty handle buffer: do_sync_h
    ' performs a real data write and retains the data on failure.)
    utils.startTest(@"A failed syncHandle reports the error and retains the data")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(4)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.clearFlushError()
    sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
    sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injFile)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(handle >= 0, @"file created", true)
    sd.writeHandle(handle, @writeBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE/2) ' Dirty the handle buffer -- THIS is what sync must land
    sd.setTestFailWriteAfter(1)                         ' The flush's data write is the next writeSector
    result := sd.syncHandle(handle)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()

    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"sync injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"syncHandle reports the failure", true)
    ' HF_DIRTY survives the failed flush, so an unimpeded retry lands the data.
    result := sd.syncHandle(handle)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"retry syncHandle succeeds -- the data was retained", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)

    ' Test 33: an aborted FAT scan must not be reported as a free-space figure. A count
    ' that stopped part-way is simply too low, and an application sizing a write against
    ' it is told something false as though it were measured.
    utils.startTest(@"An incomplete free-space scan reports an error, not a number")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(3)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    freeBefore := sd.freeSpace()                        ' The true figure, measured cleanly
    utils.evaluateSubBool(freeBefore > 0, @"a healthy scan returns a real count", true)

    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    sd.setTestFailSector(sd.debugGetFatSec(), sd.TF_READ | sd.TF_STICKY)
    freeAfter := sd.freeSpace()
    result := sd.ERROR()
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()

    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"FAT read injection actually fired", true)
    ' Zero-with-an-error cannot be mistaken for "the volume is nearly full".
    utils.evaluateSubBool(freeAfter == 0 AND result < 0, @"failed scan returns 0 AND reports why", true)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' Test 34 (v1.7.0 task #50): an aborted move must STAY aborted. moveFile stages the
    ' destination entry (F_NEWDIR) before deleting the source; if the re-read of the
    ' source entry's sector then fails, the unfixed driver returned with the staged
    ' entry still pending, and the NEXT operation's do_close() wrote it -- one file
    ' live in two directories. The fix abandons the move exactly as the source-delete
    ' WRITE failure path always did. CAN-FAIL: against the unfixed driver the ghost
    ' check below finds INJMVD/INJMVSRC.TXT.
    '
    ' Arming: setTestFailReadAfter(3) counts CARD reads (cache misses) from here --
    ' (1) do_open's root-sector search read (the create/close invalidated the dir
    ' cache), (2) do_newfile's read of the empty destination directory (do_chdir's
    ' root search is a cache hit), (3) moveFile's re-read of the source sector: the
    ' read under test. Layout assumption (holds on the sweep's reformatted card):
    ' root entries fit in the first root sector. If residue ever pushes the source
    ' entry to a later sector the injection fires during the search instead -- the
    ' move still fails, F_NEWDIR is never set, and every assertion still holds; only
    ' the exact path exercised degrades, which the hardware shakedown would show as
    ' this comment's read ordering no longer matching the debug log.
    utils.startTest(@"An aborted move stays aborted -- no ghost destination entry")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(5)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
    sd.deleteFile(@injMvChild)                          ' pre-clean, child first (populated dirs refuse delete)
    sd.deleteFile(@injMvDir)
    sd.deleteFile(@injMvSrc)
    result := sd.newDirectory(@injMvDir)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"destination directory created", 0)
    handle := sd.createFileNew(@injMvSrc)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(handle >= 0, @"source file created", true)
    sd.writeHandle(handle, @"move me", 7)
    sd.closeFileHandle(handle)

    sd.setTestFailReadAfter(3)                          ' The source-sector re-read inside moveFile (see header)
    result := sd.moveFile(@injMvSrc, @injMvDir)
    errCount := sd.getTestErrorCount()
    sd.clearTestErrors()                                ' also disarms/zeroes the read hook
    utils.evaluateSubBool(errCount > 0, @"read injection actually fired", true)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(result < 0, @"moveFile reports the failure", true)

    ' Provoke the ghost: do_delete runs do_close() first, which is exactly the call
    ' that wrote a leaked pending entry on the unfixed driver.
    sd.deleteFile(@"NOGHOST.XYZ")                       ' harmless -- the name does not exist
    handle := sd.openFileRead(@injMvChild)
    utils.evaluateSubBool(handle < 0, @"no ghost destination entry", true)
    if handle >= 0
        sd.closeFileHandle(handle)
    utils.showSubTestResults()
    ' Teardown rides the suite-end cleanup block, per suite convention.

    ' Test 35: stop() is the last chance buffered data has to reach the card, and it
    ' halts the worker immediately afterward. Its return value is the only place the
    ' outcome can still be seen.
    utils.startTest(@"stop() returns the outcome of its final unmount")
    utils.setCheckCountPerTest(2)
    sd.unmount()
    sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    result := sd.stop()
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"a clean shutdown returns SUCCESS", 0)

    ' Bring the driver back up for the cleanup below.
    result := sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"driver remounts after stop()", 0)
    utils.showSubTestResults()

    ' ======================================================================
    ' TEST GROUP: Directory Extend Reports The True Code (S11)
    ' ======================================================================
    ' When a create must grow the directory by a cluster, do_create used to
    ' flatten ANY allocateCluster failure to E_DISK_FULL -- a failed FAT write
    ' reported as a full disk. The fix propagates the allocator's true code;
    ' this group witnesses both codes through the same extension path.
    utils.startTestGroup(@"Directory Extend Reports The True Code")

    secPerClus := sd.sectorsPerCluster()
    entriesN := secPerClus * 16                          ' 32-byte entries, 16 per sector -- NEVER a constant
    ' The boundary setup is O(sectors-per-cluster) raw writes (rawFillExtDir),
    ' practical at every geometry our formatter emits (8..64 sec/clus) and at
    ' any spec-legal FAT32 cluster size up to 128 sec/clus. requireSetup still
    ' records a SCORED FAILURE, not a skip -- a geometry outside FAT32's own
    ' bounds means the mount lied, and the suite goes red and forces the
    ' question rather than silently losing the witness.
    if utils.requireSetup(entriesN > 2 AND secPerClus <= 128, @"SETUP: dir-extend boundary buildable at this geometry")

        ' Test 36: genuine disk-full via setTestMaxClusters -> E_DISK_FULL;
        ' armed FAT write -> E_IO_ERROR; disarmed retry -> success
        utils.startTest(@"Directory extend reports the allocator's true code")
        utils.setCheckCountPerTest(5)

        ' Leftover-tolerant preflight: a prior aborted run may have stranded
        ' EXTDIR (possibly already extended, which would defeat the last-slot
        ' geometry below) -- wipe its slots raw and delete it before building
        ' fresh. The raw wipe clears synthetics from THIS suite version and
        ' EXTnnn.TMP fill files from pre-redesign versions alike.
        if sd.changeDirectory(@extDirName) == 0
            rawWipeExtDir(sd.debugGetDirSec(), secPerClus)
            sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
            sd.deleteFile(@extDirName)

        result := sd.newDirectory(@extDirName)
        if result == 0
            result := sd.changeDirectory(@extDirName)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"EXTDIR created and entered", 0)
        if result == 0
            ' The subdirectory's FAT sector, derived at runtime: first sector
            ' from the CWD getter, cluster from the root-relative offset.
            ' (Assumes root at cluster 2 and 128 FAT entries/sector -- true
            ' for this driver's format; a debugFatSectorForCluster() getter
            ' would make this exact. Backlogged.)
            dataSector := sd.debugGetDirSec()                                   ' subdir's first sector (reused for the raw fill)
            testSector := (dataSector - sd.debugGetRootSec()) / secPerClus + 2  ' subdir's cluster number
            testSector := sd.debugGetFatSec() + (testSector >> 7)               ' FAT sector holding its entry

            ' Occupy every slot except the cluster's LAST ('.' and '..' hold
            ' 0-1): exactly one slot remains, so the NEXT create triggers the
            ' extension. All three witnesses below run at THIS one boundary:
            ' the capped create fails inside allocateCluster before any
            ' directory write, so the boundary survives for the armed and
            ' retry cases.
            utils.evaluateSubBool(rawFillExtDir(dataSector, secPerClus), @"filled to last-slot boundary", true)

            ' Genuine disk-full: cap the FAT at 3 clusters (only root's
            ' cluster 2 lies below the cap, and it is in use) -- the extend
            ' finds no free cluster and reports E_DISK_FULL, touching nothing
            sd.setTestMaxClusters(3)
            result := sd.createFileNew(@extWitName)
            utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"genuine disk-full extend reports E_DISK_FULL", sd.E_DISK_FULL)
            if result >= 0
                sd.closeFileHandle(result)
            sd.clearTestErrors()

            ' Armed one-shot: the extension's chain-link write to the subdir's
            ' FAT entry fails whichever FAT sector the new cluster lands in.
            ' allocateCluster maps the failed write to E_IO_ERROR -- the code
            ' that used to be swallowed by the E_DISK_FULL flattening. (A
            ' failed extension may leak 1 cluster by doctrine -- reclaimed by
            ' the sweep-preflight fsck, same as the S3/S5 bounded leaks.)
            sd.setTestFailSector(testSector, sd.TF_WRITE)
            result := sd.createFileNew(@extWitName)
            utils.evaluateSubValue(result, @"armed extend returns E_IO_ERROR", sd.E_IO_ERROR)
            if result >= 0
                sd.closeFileHandle(result)

            ' Disarmed retry: same create now lands the entry and extends
            sd.clearTestErrors()
            result := sd.createFileNew(@extWitName)
            utils.evaluateSubBool(result >= 0, @"disarmed retry succeeds", true)
            if result >= 0
                sd.closeFileHandle(result)
        else
            utils.evaluateSubBool(false, @"filled to last-slot boundary", true)
            utils.evaluateSubBool(false, @"genuine disk-full extend reports E_DISK_FULL", true)
            utils.evaluateSubBool(false, @"armed extend returns E_IO_ERROR", true)
            utils.evaluateSubBool(false, @"disarmed retry succeeds", true)
        utils.showSubTestResults()

        ' Child-first cleanup on every path -- the witness file goes by name,
        ' the synthetics go by raw wipe (which also clears any extension
        ' cluster's visibility), then the emptied directory is deleted,
        ' freeing its whole chain. Misses are free. The chdir guard keeps a
        ' failed created-and-entered path from raw-writing stale sectors.
        sd.clearTestErrors()
        if sd.changeDirectory(@extDirName) == 0
            sd.deleteFile(@extWitName)
            rawWipeExtDir(sd.debugGetDirSec(), secPerClus)
        sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
        sd.deleteFile(@extDirName)

    ' ======================================================================
    ' Cleanup and Summary
    ' ======================================================================
    sd.clearFlushError()
    sd.clearTestErrors()
    cleanupTestItems()

    sd.unmount()

    utils.ShowTestEndCounts()

    debug(" ")
    debug("END_SESSION")

PRI fillSlot(pEntry)
' Stamp one synthetic occupied directory entry: an empty 8.3 file
' ("ZZFILLER", attr $20, first-cluster 0, size 0) -- the same shape the
' driver itself leaves for a lazily-allocated empty file, so every scan
' path parses it.
'
' @param pEntry - Pointer to the 32-byte entry slot to stamp

    bytefill(pEntry, 0, 32)
    bytemove(pEntry, @fillEntryName, 11)
    BYTE[pEntry + 11] := $20

PRI rawFillExtDir(dirSec, nSec) : bOk | si, ei
' Drive the CWD's first cluster to its last free slot by RAW sector writes:
' every entry slot except the cluster's final one becomes an occupied
' synthetic. O(sectors-per-cluster) card writes, so the boundary setup is
' practical at EVERY supported geometry -- the create-loop fill this
' replaces was O(entries^2) directory-scan work, impractical past 16
' sec/clus, and its guard went red-by-design on big-cluster cards.
' Sector 0 keeps '.' and '..'. Raw writes route through writeSector, which
' invalidates every cache tier holding these sectors.
'
' @param dirSec - First sector of the directory's first cluster
' @param nSec - Sectors per cluster
' @returns bOk - TRUE only if every raw read/write succeeded

    if sd.readSectorRaw(dirSec, @sectorBuffer) < 0
        return false
    repeat ei from 2 to 15
        if nSec > 1 OR ei < 15
            fillSlot(@sectorBuffer + (ei << 5))
    if sd.writeSectorRaw(dirSec, @sectorBuffer) < 0
        return false
    if nSec > 1
        repeat si from 1 to nSec - 1
            bytefill(@sectorBuffer, 0, 512)
            repeat ei from 0 to 15
                if si < nSec - 1 OR ei < 15
                    fillSlot(@sectorBuffer + (ei << 5))
            if sd.writeSectorRaw(dirSec + si, @sectorBuffer) < 0
                return false
    bOk := true

PRI rawWipeExtDir(dirSec, nSec) | si
' Reverse rawFillExtDir: zero every slot after '.' and '..' so the directory
' enumerates as empty and deleteFile() can remove it, freeing the whole
' chain including any extension cluster Test 36 grew. Also clears EXTnnn.TMP
' fill entries left by pre-redesign suite versions. Failures are tolerated
' -- cleanup is best-effort by convention.

    if sd.readSectorRaw(dirSec, @sectorBuffer) == 0
        bytefill(@sectorBuffer + 64, 0, 512 - 64)
        sd.writeSectorRaw(dirSec, @sectorBuffer)
    if nSec > 1
        bytefill(@sectorBuffer, 0, 512)
        repeat si from 1 to nSec - 1
            sd.writeSectorRaw(dirSec + si, @sectorBuffer)

PRI cleanupTestItems()
' Delete every fixture this suite creates -- or could leak on a regressed driver.
' Called twice per sibling-suite convention: right after mount, so residue from
' an aborted prior run cannot poison this one (a leftover TIER1GD, for example,
' turns test 18's healthy newDirectory into E_FILE_EXISTS with zero writes),
' and again as suite teardown. Every delete is defensive: a missing name costs
' one failed directory search. Children go before their directories -- populated
' directories refuse delete.

  sd.changeDirectory(@rootPath)
  sd.deleteFile(@injFile)
  sd.deleteFile(@crossA)
  sd.deleteFile(@crossB)
  sd.deleteFile(@renFile)
  sd.deleteFile(@renNewName)
  sd.deleteFile(@mvSrcFile)
  sd.deleteFile(@injMvSrc)
  sd.deleteFile(@injMvChild)                            ' Test 34's ghost entry, child first
  sd.deleteFile(@injMvDir)
  sd.deleteFile(@mvDestChild)                           ' Test 20: a regressed moveFile leaves the copy inside
  sd.deleteFile(@dirMoveDest)
  sd.deleteFile(@dirGood)
  sd.deleteFile(@dirBad)                                ' Its creation is DESIGNED to fail (test 18)
  sd.deleteFile(@dirFull)                               ' Its creation is DESIGNED to fail (test 16)

PRI discoverDataSector(fileOffset) : sector | handle
' Find the LBA of the data sector holding a given offset of the test file.
'
' No accessor reports where a file's data lives, so this measures it: read that
' offset on a healthy card, then ask the read diagnostic which sector the read
' just touched. The remount first makes the read a genuine card access rather
' than a cache hit, so the diagnostic reflects this read and not an earlier one.
'
' @param fileOffset - Byte offset within the test file
' @returns sector - LBA of the sector containing that offset
' @local handle - Read handle used for the probing read

  sd.unmount()
  sd.mount(SD_CS, SD_MOSI, SD_MISO, SD_SCK)
  handle := sd.openFileRead(@injFile)
  if fileOffset > 0
    sd.seekHandle(handle, fileOffset)
  sd.readHandle(handle, @readBuffer, BUFFER_SIZE)
  sd.debugGetReadSectorDiag(@diagLastSector, @diagScratch[0], @diagScratch[1], @diagScratch[2], @diagScratch[3], @diagScratch[4], @diagScratch[5], @diagScratch[6])
  sector := diagLastSector
  sd.closeFileHandle(handle)

